From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 23:28:45 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] winex11: no outputs right now is not a reason to give up on
 XRandR for good.

X11DRV_XRandR_Init looked for a connected output and, finding none, returned
without installing the display handler at all.  That threw away the RandR
change events along with it, and those are the only way a process would ever
learn that outputs had come back.  A process that happened to start while the
screen was away stayed blind to the display for the rest of its life.

Which is not a corner case here: the session's monitor is a virtual output
from gnome-remote-desktop, so it exists only while a client is connected.
Every disconnect took the output away, and every Office started afterwards was
stuck with no display it could ever recover from.  A monitor switched off, a
laptop lid, an unplugged dock all do the same thing.

Install the handler either way.  With nothing connected it reports no adapters,
which is the truth, and win32u stands in a virtual monitor until the real ones
are back; the change handler then re-reads the display by force.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit d245c47506fdd40cc244e55c993696f754272b85)

diff --git a/dlls/winex11.drv/xrandr.c b/dlls/winex11.drv/xrandr.c
index b147b952cab..5cfebe67084 100644
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/xrandr.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/xrandr.c
@@ -1722,11 +1722,20 @@ void X11DRV_XRandR_Init(void)
         }
         pXRRFreeScreenResources( screen_resources );
 
+        /* Outputs come and go: a monitor switched off, a laptop lid closed, a
+         * dock unplugged, a Wayland compositor that takes its output away
+         * while the screen sleeps.  Finding none is a statement about this
+         * moment, not about the server, so it is no reason to give up on
+         * RandR for the rest of the process's life -- and giving up takes the
+         * change events with it, which are the only way outputs coming back
+         * would ever be noticed.  A process that happened to start while the
+         * screen was asleep stayed blind to the display for good.
+         *
+         * Install the handler either way.  With nothing connected it reports
+         * no adapters, which is the truth, and win32u stands in a virtual
+         * monitor until the real ones are back. */
         if (!found_output)
-        {
-            WARN("No connected outputs found.\n");
-            return;
-        }
+            WARN("No connected outputs at the moment, keeping XRandR for when there are.\n");
 
         display_handler.name = "XRandR 1.4";
         display_handler.priority = 200;
